\chapter{Where nytid keeps its files, the \texttt{home} module}%
\label{home}

nytid writes to several places, and until this module each part of it
found its own:

\begin{description}
\item[The configuration] [[typerconf]] keeps it in
  [[~/.config/nytid/config.json]], a name it derives from the running
  program's name.
\item[The todo store] [[~/.nytid/todos]] (\cref{cli.todo}), unless
  [[todo.data_dir]] names another directory.
\item[The tracking store] [[~/.nytid/tracking]] (\cref{cli.track}),
  unless [[track.data_dir]] does.
\item[Smaller stores] the calendar cache [[~/.nytid/ics_cache]] of
  [[nytid.schedules]], the [[mine]] course register
  [[~/.nytid/mine]] (\cref{mine}) and the hourly agent job's
  [[agent.log]].
\item[Runtime state] the tmux-scoped window and pane state, in
  [[nytid-<uid>]] under the temporary directory
  (\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}).
\end{description}

Running nytid against throw-away data---to try a new version, in a
script, in an experiment---therefore meant knowing every one of these
and redirecting each.  Missing one is not a theoretical risk.  Scripts
that redirected only the todo directory still rewrote the user's real
tracking files, because finishing a todo also stops its time tracking,
and that went through the tracking store the scripts had not moved
(issue \#560).

This module gives all of them one root.  When the environment variable
[[NYTID_HOME]] is set, it replaces [[~/.nytid]] under every default
above, it holds the configuration as [[$NYTID_HOME/config.json]], and
the runtime state gets a directory of its own.  When it is unset,
nothing changes.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
  & [[NYTID_HOME]] unset & [[NYTID_HOME=/x]] \\
  \hline
  configuration & [[~/.config/nytid/config.json]] & [[/x/config.json]] \\
  todos & [[~/.nytid/todos]] & [[/x/todos]] \\
  tracking & [[~/.nytid/tracking]] & [[/x/tracking]] \\
  calendar cache & [[~/.nytid/ics_cache]] & [[/x/ics_cache]] \\
  [[mine]] register & [[~/.nytid/mine]] & [[/x/mine]] \\
  tmux state & [[$TMPDIR/nytid-<uid>]] & [[$TMPDIR/nytid-<uid>-<digest>]] \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The modules keep their configuration keys: [[todo.data_dir]] still
wins over the default, read from the configuration in the home when
the variable is set.
We first look at the variable itself, then at the data root built on
it, then at the configuration---the one location that needs more than
a different default---and finally at the runtime state and at what the
variable does not reach.

\section{Module structure}

The module imports nothing beyond the cheap parts of the standard
library at load time.  That matters because importing the [[nytid]]
package runs it (\cref{sec:home-import}), and [[prompt-nytid]] imports
the package on every shell prompt (\cref{prompt}).

<<[[home.py]]>>=
"""Where nytid keeps its configuration and data.

Every default location nytid writes derives from `data_dir()` or
`config_file()` here, so setting NYTID_HOME moves them all.  See the
`home` chapter in the manual for the design rationale.
"""
import os
import pathlib
import sys

<<constants>>
<<functions>>
