\section{The configuration}

Moving the data defaults is not enough on its own, and the reason is
the configuration.  It may name the data directories explicitly---the
author's own configuration sets [[todo.data_dir]] to
[[~/.nytid/todos]]---and an
explicit key wins over any default.  With only the defaults moved, a
sandboxed command would read the real configuration, find the real
directory in it and write there.  So the configuration has to move
with the data: under [[NYTID_HOME]], nytid reads and writes
[[$NYTID_HOME/config.json]] and never the user's own file.  A key set
in \emph{that} file still wins, as it should: a home is a complete
installation of nytid, with its own settings.

The name of the file is fixed.  [[typerconf]] names its directory after
the running program, [[sys.argv[0]]], so [[nytid]] reads
[[~/.config/nytid]] while a script that imports nytid reads a
directory named after the script, and [[prompt-nytid]] has to
hard-code [[nytid]] to agree with the CLI (\cref{prompt:config}).
Inside a home every program finds the same file.

<<constants>>=
CONFIG_FILE_NAME = "config.json"
<<functions>>=
def config_file():
    """Return the configuration file in $NYTID_HOME.

    Returns None when NYTID_HOME is unset; typerconf's own
    location then stands.
    """
    home = nytid_home()
    if home is None:
        return None
    return home / CONFIG_FILE_NAME
@

Let's verify where the file lands, and that without the variable we
leave the choice to [[typerconf]].

<<test functions>>=
def test_config_file_lives_in_the_home(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
    """The configuration moves only when NYTID_HOME is set."""
    monkeypatch.delenv(NYTID_HOME_ENV, raising=False)
    assert config_file() is None
    monkeypatch.setenv(NYTID_HOME_ENV, str(tmp_path))
    assert config_file() == tmp_path / "config.json"
@

\subsection{Redirecting [[typerconf]]}

Knowing the file is the easy half; [[typerconf]] has to use it.  It
offers no setting for that.  It computes its directory once, when it
is imported, and bakes the resulting path into the \emph{default
arguments} of [[Config.read_config]] and [[Config.write_config]].
Every configuration access in nytid ends in one of those two
defaults: the [[nytid config]] command, the module-level
[[typerconf.get]] and [[typerconf.set]], and the cached [[default]]
helper (\cref{cli.utils.defaults.cache}).  Changing the environment
cannot reach a value that has already been computed, so we overwrite
the captured default itself.  The test suite has always isolated
itself this way (\cref{sec:isolating-the-config}); it now uses this
function.

Overwriting a default blindly would be a poor trade.  If a future
[[typerconf]] moved the path out of the first slot, the assignment
would still succeed, and nytid would quietly go back to the user's
real configuration---the one outcome the variable exists to rule out.
So we check what we replace and refuse to guess.  The error stops
nytid from starting at all, which is the safe direction.

<<functions>>=
def replace_config_default(method, conf_file):
    """Make `conf_file` the default config path of `method`.

    Raises RuntimeError if `method` does not capture a config
    path as its first default argument.
    """
    defaults = method.__defaults__
    if not defaults or not str(defaults[0]).endswith(
        CONFIG_FILE_NAME
    ):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"cannot honour {NYTID_HOME_ENV}: "
            f"typerconf.{method.__qualname__} no longer "
            f"captures its config path as its first default "
            f"argument"
        )
    method.__defaults__ = (str(conf_file),) + defaults[1:]
@

We also replace [[typerconf.dirs]], the [[platformdirs]] object the
path was computed from, for any code that asks it later.  Every
attribute of a [[platformdirs.AppDirs]] that ends in [[_dir]] names a
directory, so a stand-in answering all of them with the home keeps
those lookups inside it too.

<<functions>>=
class HomeDirs:
    """Stand-in for `platformdirs.AppDirs` that resolves every
    directory to one root."""

    def __init__(self, root):
        self.root = str(root)

    def __getattr__(self, name):
        if name.endswith("_dir"):
            return self.root
        raise AttributeError(name)


def redirect_config(conf_file):
    """Make typerconf read and write `conf_file` by default."""
    import typerconf

    typerconf.dirs = HomeDirs(pathlib.Path(conf_file).parent)
    for method in (
        typerconf.Config.read_config,
        typerconf.Config.write_config,
    ):
        replace_config_default(method, conf_file)
@

Let's verify the redirection where it matters: a value set through
[[typerconf]] lands in our file and is read back from it.  The test
suite has redirected [[typerconf]] already, so a fixture puts the
suite's redirection back afterwards.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.fixture
def restore_typerconf():
    """Undo a test's redirection of typerconf."""
    saved_dirs = typerconf.dirs
    methods = (
        typerconf.Config.read_config,
        typerconf.Config.write_config,
    )
    saved = [method.__defaults__ for method in methods]
    yield
    typerconf.dirs = saved_dirs
    for method, defaults in zip(methods, saved):
        method.__defaults__ = defaults


def test_redirect_config_moves_reads_and_writes(
    tmp_path, restore_typerconf
):
    """typerconf.set and typerconf.get use the new file."""
    conf_file = tmp_path / "config.json"
    redirect_config(conf_file)

    typerconf.set("todo.data_dir", "/somewhere")

    assert json.loads(conf_file.read_text()) == {
        "todo": {"data_dir": "/somewhere"}
    }
    assert typerconf.get("todo.data_dir") == "/somewhere"
    assert typerconf.dirs.user_config_dir == str(tmp_path)
