And the refusal: a method whose first default is not a configuration
path is left alone, and we say why.

<<test functions>>=
def test_replace_config_default_refuses_to_guess():
    """An unexpected signature is an error, not a guess."""
    def read_config(self, verbose=False):
        pass

    with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match="NYTID_HOME"):
        replace_config_default(read_config, "/x/config.json")
    assert read_config.__defaults__ == (False,)
@

\subsection{When the redirection happens}%
\label{sec:home-import}

The redirection must be in place before anything reads the
configuration, and some of nytid reads it while it is being imported:
the [[nytid config]] command loads the file when the CLI builds its
commands.  Nor is the CLI the only way in.  [[prompt-nytid]] imports
[[nytid.prompt]], and a script may import [[nytid.cli.todo]] or
[[nytid.courses.registry]] directly.  What all of them have in common
is that Python imports the [[nytid]] package first.  So the package's
[[__init__.py]] calls the function below, and every entry point,
present or future, is covered before its first module body runs.

That file is the package marker, written by hand rather than tangled,
so it stays a single call to this function:

\begin{minted}{python}
from nytid.home import apply_nytid_home

apply_nytid_home()
\end{minted}

Redirecting right there would be too early, though, and the prompt
would pay for it.  The redirection imports [[typerconf]], which imports
[[typer]]: about sixty milliseconds, several times what
[[prompt-nytid]] spends on a whole prompt, and the prompt never reads
the configuration through [[typerconf]] at all
(\cref{prompt:config}).  So we redirect at the last possible moment
instead, just after [[typerconf]] itself has been imported.

If it already has been---a script imported it before nytid, as the
test suite does---we redirect at once.  Otherwise we put a finder at
the front of [[sys.meta_path]], the list of objects Python asks to
locate a module that is imported for the first time.  For any other
name the finder declines, and Python asks the next one.  For
[[typerconf]] it removes itself, lets the standard machinery locate
the module, and hands back the result with the loader wrapped.  The
wrapper runs the real loader, which executes [[typerconf]]'s module
body and so computes its default path, and then the redirection---all
before the [[import]] statement that asked for [[typerconf]] returns.
No code can see [[typerconf]] unredirected, and a process that never
imports it never pays for it.

<<functions>>=
class RedirectOnImport:
    """Import-system finder that redirects typerconf once it
    has been imported."""

    def __init__(self, conf_file):
        self.conf_file = conf_file

    def find_spec(self, name, path=None, target=None):
        if name != "typerconf":
            return None
        sys.meta_path.remove(self)
        import importlib.util

        spec = importlib.util.find_spec(name)
        if spec is not None:
            spec.loader = RedirectingLoader(
                spec.loader, self.conf_file
            )
        return spec
@

The wrapper puts the real loader back on the module before running
it, so that to [[typerconf]] and to [[importlib]] the module looks
exactly as if it had been imported the ordinary way.

<<functions>>=
class RedirectingLoader:
    """Loader that runs typerconf's real loader, then
    redirects it."""

    def __init__(self, loader, conf_file):
        self.loader = loader
        self.conf_file = conf_file

    def create_module(self, spec):
        return self.loader.create_module(spec)

    def exec_module(self, module):
        module.__loader__ = module.__spec__.loader = self.loader
        self.loader.exec_module(module)
        redirect_config(self.conf_file)
@

A process may apply the variable more than once; the tests do.  A
second finder would never fire, since the first one's import puts
[[typerconf]] in [[sys.modules]], where Python looks before asking any
finder, so we keep a single finder and give it the newest file.

<<functions>>=
def redirect_when_imported(conf_file):
    """Redirect typerconf to `conf_file` once it is imported."""
    for finder in sys.meta_path:
        if isinstance(finder, RedirectOnImport):
            finder.conf_file = conf_file
            return
    sys.meta_path.insert(0, RedirectOnImport(conf_file))
@

The function the package calls ties the two cases together.  It also
writes the absolute form of the variable back into the environment.
The processes nytid starts---the launched command of a todo, a shell
in its working directory---inherit the environment, not our current
directory, so a relative value would name a different directory for
them (\cref{home}).

<<functions>>=
def apply_nytid_home():
    """Honour NYTID_HOME in this process and the ones it starts.

    Does nothing when NYTID_HOME is unset or empty.
    """
    home = nytid_home()
    if home is None:
        return
    os.environ[NYTID_HOME_ENV] = str(home)
    if "typerconf" in sys.modules:
        redirect_config(config_file())
    else:
        redirect_when_imported(config_file())
@

Let's verify both effects in this process, where [[typerconf]] is
already imported, and that applying twice leaves one finder waiting
for a later import.

<<test functions>>=
def test_apply_nytid_home_pins_the_absolute_home(
    monkeypatch, tmp_path, restore_typerconf
):
    """A relative home is exported absolute and holds the config."""
    monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path)
    monkeypatch.setenv(NYTID_HOME_ENV, "sandbox")

    apply_nytid_home()

    assert os.environ[NYTID_HOME_ENV] == str(tmp_path / "sandbox")
    assert typerconf.Config.read_config.__defaults__[0] == str(
        tmp_path / "sandbox" / "config.json"
    )


def test_redirect_when_imported_keeps_one_finder(monkeypatch):
    """A second application updates the waiting finder."""
    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "meta_path", list(sys.meta_path))

    redirect_when_imported("/a/config.json")
    redirect_when_imported("/b/config.json")

    finders = [
        finder for finder in sys.meta_path
        if isinstance(finder, RedirectOnImport)
    ]
    assert [finder.conf_file for finder in finders] == [
        "/b/config.json"
    ]
@

In-process tests cannot show that the import really does it, because
the test suite imported nytid and [[typerconf]] long before.  A fresh
process can.  The first one imports nytid and then [[typerconf]], the
order in which the CLI does it, and reports what [[typerconf]] ended
up with: the file in the home, and its own loader.

<<test functions>>=
def test_typerconf_imported_after_nytid_is_redirected(tmp_path):
    """The finder redirects a later import of typerconf."""
    probe = (
        "import sys, nytid;"
        "assert 'typerconf' not in sys.modules;"
        "import typerconf;"
        "print(typerconf.Config.read_config.__defaults__[0]);"
        "print(type(typerconf.__loader__).__name__)"
    )
    env = dict(
        os.environ,
        NYTID_HOME=str(tmp_path),
        PYTHONPATH=str(pathlib.Path(nytid.__file__).parents[1]),
    )
    result = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", probe], env=env,
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=True,
    )
    conf_file, loader = result.stdout.split()
    assert conf_file == str(tmp_path / "config.json")
    assert loader != "RedirectingLoader"
