\section{Runtime state}

The tmux state does not move into the home.  It lives in the temporary
directory so that a reboot clears it together with the windows it
describes (\cref{track:tmux-state-dir}), and a home is meant to
survive reboots.  But it cannot stay in the shared [[nytid-<uid>]]
either: a sandboxed command run in the user's tmux would then rewrite
the window labels the user's real tracking relies on.  So a home gets
its own directory next to it, named by a digest of the home's path.
The digest keeps the name short and free of the slashes a path would
bring, and the same home always gets the same directory.  We import
[[hashlib]] on this branch only: it costs a couple of milliseconds that
a prompt without [[NYTID_HOME]] has no reason to pay.

<<functions>>=
def tmux_state_dirname():
    """Return the name of the tmux-state directory.

    The directory lives in the temporary directory: nytid-<uid>,
    with a digest of $NYTID_HOME appended when that is set.
    """
    name = f"nytid-{os.getuid()}"
    home = nytid_home()
    if home is None:
        return name
    import hashlib

    digest = hashlib.sha256(
        str(home).encode()
    ).hexdigest()[:12]
    return f"{name}-{digest}"
@

Let's verify that homes are kept apart and that a home keeps its
directory.

<<test functions>>=
def test_tmux_state_dirname_separates_homes(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
    """Each home has its own state directory, the default none."""
    monkeypatch.delenv(NYTID_HOME_ENV, raising=False)
    plain = tmux_state_dirname()
    assert plain == f"nytid-{os.getuid()}"

    names = []
    for home in ("a", "b", "a"):
        monkeypatch.setenv(NYTID_HOME_ENV, str(tmp_path / home))
        names.append(tmux_state_dirname())
    assert names[0] == names[2]
    assert len({plain, names[0], names[1]}) == 3
    assert all(name.startswith(plain + "-") for name in names)
@


\section{What the variable does not reach}

Two kinds of location stay where they are.

\begin{description}
\item[Locations named in the configuration] The [[hr]] directories,
  course registers and similar keys name places outside nytid's own
  tree, often shared ones in AFS.  In a fresh home they are simply
  not configured; a home whose configuration names a shared
  directory writes there, as it would without the variable.
\item[The tmux hooks] Tracking installs hooks in the tmux server that
  run [[nytid]] on window and pane events.  Those commands run in the
  tmux server's environment, not in the one that installed them, so
  they do not see [[NYTID_HOME]].  A sandbox that starts tracked
  tmux sessions inside the user's own tmux server therefore still
  reaches the user's data through those hooks; such a sandbox needs
  its own tmux server as well ([[tmux -L sandbox]]).
\end{description}

